{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For any leaven and any honey you shall not offer as incense, etc.; an offering of first fruits you shall bring them to HaShem, but they shall not go up onto the altar for a pleasing fragrance.</b> Rashi explained that the two loaves of Shavuot come from leaven and the first fruits come from honey. The explanation of the matter: every person has desire for all the cherished things of this world, which are designated by honey — for just as honey is sweet to the palate yet its abundance is harmful, so all the cherished things of this world are necessary yet their abundance is harmful. And this is the proper counsel: that a person should use what is necessary and consecrate the surplus. And the leaven is a model for the evil inclination, as R. Alexandri said in his prayer (Berakhot 17a): 'Our will is to do Your will, but the leaven in the dough holds us back.' And these two are necessary for the existence of man — for if he does not use his necessary needs, which are designated by honey, he will die and not live, and his limbs will not be strong and healthy to exert themselves in the commandments of HaShem. And were it not for the evil inclination, a man would not marry a wife and would not build a house, and the world would be found destroyed. And these two precede in time the occupation with Torah and commandments — for if he does not eat flour first, there is no Torah; but the occupation with Torah is first in thought and in precedence, because the leaven and the honey that we mentioned have no perfection from their own side to go up as a pleasing fragrance to HaShem, except that they are a first and a beginning for man, through which he can come to the perfection of the soul." "Therefore it says, 'Any leaven and any honey you shall not offer from it as incense to HaShem.' For from their own side they have no aspect of perfection that would go up as a pleasing fragrance to HaShem; but 'an offering of first fruits you shall bring them' — to teach that they are a first and a beginning for man, to bring him to the true perfection that is impossible to attain without them. And for this reason one offers from them only the leaven as an offering of first fruits on the day of the giving of the Torah — for the Torah is like a spice to the evil inclination, and the two loaves of Shavuot were of leaven, for the Torah that was given in it shall be 'healing to your navel' (Proverbs 3:8), and the first fruits come from honey — for at the time that one gives to HaShem the first of the fruit of his land from every food that shall be eaten, then he will eat all his days ordinary food that has been prepared in the purity of the holy, for this indicates that his eating is with this intention — that the true perfection is first in his thought. And so too the reason for the first fruits is to break his desire, as will be explained, God willing, in the portion of Ki Tavo."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:05.896581237Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}